{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Judah</b> — masculine and feminine, like Israel and Egypt, \"and Egypt grew strong\". Some interpret Egypt from the words of the transmitters of the tradition, of blessed memory: \"the law of the neighbor.\" What seems right to me is \"from the straits,\" though it is slightly irregular, and the resh properly takes a dagesh. Thus its path is masculine and feminine, like \"house\" and \"place\":"], :book :lamentations, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Genesis 41:35 (ותחזק מצרים) where מצרים is vocalized as מִצְרַיִם (straits), a feminine noun distinct from the country-name. The Aramaic dictum דינא דבר מצרא (the law of the neighbor) is from b. Bava Batra 12a. The unquoted end of the verse in Lamentations 1:3 is מן מצר, supplying the form Ibn Ezra then discusses."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Lamentations -- Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%9E%D7%92%D7%99%D7%9C%D7%AA_%D7%90%D7%99%D7%9B%D7%94"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:07:24.683570174Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}